#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128929 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0559f0c-a1ca-5ba8-a02e-71d584273958" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Using Lower and Upper Riemann Sums"}] :question/id #uuid "7caffa88-776d-52e1-8b81-2ebf98aedebc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6122" :question/problem "Let $R = [-1,4]\\times [1,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$\\displaystyle\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}A$,\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that $m_{ij} = M_{ij} = 3$ for all partitions $P$ of $R$, and $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion $R_{ij}$." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y)$, the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R$, where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)m_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j} \\\\\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)M_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$.\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nm_{ij}=M_{ij}=3\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(f, P) = U(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)3Δx_{i}Δy_{j} \\\\\n&= 3(∑_(i = 1)^(m)Δx_{i})(∑_(j = 1)^(n)Δy_{j}) \\\\\n&= 3(4 - (-1))(2 - 1) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nL(f, P) = 15 \\mid \\le ∬_(R)f(x, y)dA \\le 15 = U(f, P)\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d A = 15\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128974 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b9d2e98-8007-51ec-bfb6-cb9b48029a2a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Using the Mean Value of X and Y"}] :question/id #uuid "3464909c-5b04-5cb1-96c9-a409fa105bb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9494" :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R$.\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n$L(f, P)|= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(x_{i - 1} + 2y_{j - 1})Δx_{i}Δy_{j},; U(f, P)|= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(x_{i} + 2y_{j})Δx_{i}Δy_{j}$.\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P)$, compute $\\displaystyle\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d A$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n$\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left(x_{i-1} + x_i\\right)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j = (x_m^2 - x_0^2)(y_n-y_0)$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y)$, the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R$.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\bar{x}_i = \\dfrac 1 2 (x_{i-1}+x_i)\n$$\n\nwill always lie between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$, and similarly\n\n$$\n\\bar{y}_j = \\dfrac 1 2 (y_{j-1}+y_j)\n$$\n\nwill always lie between $y_{j-1}$ and $y_j$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx_{i-1} + {\\color{blue}2}y_{j-1} \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2}(x_{i-1} + x_i) + {\\color{blue}2}\\cdot \\dfrac 1 2 (y_{j-1} + y_j) \\leq x_{i} + {\\color{blue}2}y_{j}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\nx_{i-1} + 2y_{j-1} \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2}(x_{i-1} + x_i) + (y_{j-1} + y_j)\\leq x_{i} + 2y_{j}\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $\\Delta x_i\\Delta y_j$ and applying the double summation gives\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left[\\dfrac{1}{2}(x_{i-1} + x_i) + (y_{j-1} + y_j)\\right]\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j\\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left(x_{i-1} + x_i\\right)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j + \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left(y_{j-1} + y_j\\right)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j\\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(x_{i - 1} + x_{i})Δx_{i}Δy_{j} &= \\frac{1}{2}(2^{2} - 0^{2})(3 - 1) = 4 \\\\\n∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(y_{j - 1} + y_{j})Δx_{i}Δy_{j} &= (3^{2} - 1^{2})(2 - 0) = 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, then:\n\n$$\nL(f, P) \\le 4 + 16 \\le U(f, P)⟹L(f, P) \\le 20 \\le U(f, P)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d A = 20\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128975 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89023510-57f4-52f9-90ed-eae07f48f707" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of the Double Integral in Terms of Lower and Upper Riemann Sums"}] :question/id #uuid "e75f2d8e-c165-5a24-942b-7326d7f11f0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9497" :question/problem "Let $R = [0,1]\\times [1,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 12-x-y$. Further, let\n\n$\\displaystyle I = \\iint\\limits_{R} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm dA$\n\nbe the unique number such that over all rectangular partitions $P$ of $R$, we have\n$L(f,P)\\:\\leq I \\:\\leq U(f,P)$,\nwhere $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P)$ are the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums. Write down the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P)$." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y)$, the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R$, where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)m_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j} \\\\\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)M_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion $R_{ij}$.\n\nWe need to compute $U(f,P)$.\n\nNotice that for all subregions $R_{ij}$, we have that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 12-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = 12-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. Therefore,\n\n$$\nM_{ij} = f(x_{i-1}, y_{j-1}) = 12-x_{i-1}-y_{j-1}\n$$\n\nand the upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (12-x_{i-1}-y_{j-1})\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89023510-57f4-52f9-90ed-eae07f48f707" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of the Double Integral in Terms of Lower and Upper Riemann Sums"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d717d9c-0eb4-5131-86ae-5e4b8f971afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{i} + 2y_{j - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125949} {:answer/id #uuid "e363426c-a323-5221-9bb4-0ec402d943e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{i - 1} + 2y_{j}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125950} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a7b0c5-5385-547c-8536-1670f2c8b718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{i} + 2y_{j}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125951} {:answer/id #uuid "554de992-079d-5725-9c03-00791f0d82eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the options is correct" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125952} {:answer/id #uuid "5de4d171-f6cc-5b73-b912-9fbb6401a86c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{i - 1} + 2y_{j - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5a7b0c5-5385-547c-8536-1670f2c8b718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{i} + 2y_{j}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125951} :answer-field/id #uuid "e062ed5e-65af-537d-aae7-bfc420fc0e55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125948}] :question/id #uuid "157470f8-e24e-5ffe-9569-91b340dbb848" :question/math-academy-id "q-123489" :question/problem "Let $R = [0, 2] \\cdot [1, 3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = x + 2y$. Further, let\n\n$I = ∬_(R)f(x, y)dA$\n\nbe the unique number such that over all rectangular partitions $P$ of $R$, we have\n$L(f, P) \\le I \\le U(f, P)$,\nwhere $L(f, P)$ and $U(f, P)$ are the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums, and\n\n$U(f, P) = ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)AAAAAΔx_{i}Δy_{j}$.\n\nWhat expression is missing from the double summation above?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b9d2e98-8007-51ec-bfb6-cb9b48029a2a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Using the Mean Value of X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83bc6494-39a4-57ae-bad9-645b0de147df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128535} {:answer/id #uuid "0b0a3343-a2a3-5d10-89f0-c9a4b7913fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128536} {:answer/id #uuid "231fbeea-c7bd-52f1-a3f3-0d69514d8fbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128537} {:answer/id #uuid "54bf34d6-0d85-594b-8d04-0cf4b5a5fe37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128538} {:answer/id #uuid "587b0d87-0f2a-5589-baa7-43334248e718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54bf34d6-0d85-594b-8d04-0cf4b5a5fe37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128538} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cfe3b51-b817-5aad-bf0b-61e7a4be688d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128534}] :question/id #uuid "08870289-b60b-524f-b96e-8c75f1f41bc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-72627" :question/problem "Let $R = [0, 4] \\cdot [0, 2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y)$ be a function defined over $R$.\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$L(f, P)|= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(4x_{i - 1} + 2y_{j - 1})Δx_{i}Δy_{j},; U(f, P)|= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(4x_{i} + 2y_{j})Δx_{i}Δy_{j}$.\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f, P)$ and $U(f, P)$, compute $∬_(R)f(x, y)dA$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n$∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(x_{i - 1} + x_{i})Δx_{i}Δy_{j} = (x_{m}^{2} - x_{0}^{2})(y_{n} - y_{0})$"}]}
